<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Faynan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Feinan, Wadi Faynan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Faynan is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-rich district on the eastern side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabah">Arabah</a> valley in southern Jordan. Mined from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> onwards, it reached peaks of production in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, when the smelting site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khirbat-en-nahas">Khirbat en-Nahas</a> flourished, and in the Roman period, when convicts worked the mines at Phaeno. Its slag heaps are among the largest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/faynan.jpg" alt="Faynan" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joe Roe · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wadi_Feynan,_May_2014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
